<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Jin (936-947)</h1><p class="st-meta">936 CE – 947 CE · capital Kaifeng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The third of the Five Dynasties, founded with Khitan help at the price of the Sixteen Prefectures, a loss that shaped China's northern frontier for four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> general and son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang">Emperor Mingzong</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a>, rebelled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> in 936. He won the support of the Khitan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-deguang">Yelü Deguang</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> by promising him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a> around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a>, and with Khitan troops overthrew the Later Tang.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a> called himself 'son' of the Khitan emperor and paid annual tribute, which shamed many officials. He moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>. The cession gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> the passes through the mountains north of the plain, leaving later Chinese dynasties exposed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-chonggui">Shi Chonggui</a>, who succeeded in 942, refused to remain a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The Khitan invaded repeatedly and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> at the start of 947, deporting him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-zhiyuan">Liu Zhiyuan</a>, governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, then founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-han">Later Han</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-jin-936-947.jpg" alt="Later Jin (936-947)" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Later_Jin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>936 CE – 947 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaifeng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shi Jingtang (Emperor Gaozu), Shi Chonggui</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Later Jin (Five Dynasties), Jin (Shi Jingtang)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=942&amp;focus=later-jin-936-947" aria-label="Later Jin (936-947) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7487 816 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Jin (936-947) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7807 1072L7898 1125M7807 1072L7866 1006M7807 1072L7901 1174"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7898 1125h0M7866 1006h0M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7807 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7807 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=942&amp;focus=later-jin-936-947">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
